# Tooling — how each agent reaches Figma and Canva

Verified on this machine 2026-09-01. `bin/preflight.sh` re-checks all of it.

| | Figma | Canva |
|---|---|---|
| **Claude Code** | official remote MCP `https://mcp.figma.com/mcp` via the `figma` plugin — read *and* write (`use_figma`, `create_new_file`, `generate_figma_design`) | official remote MCP `https://mcp.canva.com/mcp` (claude.ai connector) |
| **Codex** | official remote MCP through the ChatGPT Figma connector — `figma.use_figma` is set to `approval_mode = "approve"` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so unattended runs need `--approve-for-me` (which implies workspace-write — passing `-s` as well is rejected) | official remote MCP, registered with `codex mcp add canva --url …` + `codex mcp login canva` |
| **Kimi Code CLI** | **plugin bridge only** — `~/fractal/figma-bridge` (`cursor-talk-to-figma-mcp`), 44 tools, needs a websocket and a Figma plugin running | official remote MCP via the `mcp-remote` stdio bridge (Kimi's own OAuth client fails; see below) |

## The Figma asymmetry — record it in every report

Figma's remote MCP server only accepts clients on its published catalog
(Claude, Claude Code, ChatGPT, Codex, Cursor, VS Code, Zed, Warp, and about
fifteen others). Kimi is not on it, and the registration endpoint proves it:

    POST https://api.figma.com/v1/oauth/mcp/register  →  403 Forbidden

That is a deliberate allowlist, not a misconfiguration, and no client-side
flag gets around it. Kimi therefore reaches Figma through the open-source
plugin bridge instead. What that changes:

- the bridge writes into **whichever file the plugin is running in**, so a
  human must create the empty file and start the plugin before the run;
- it exposes primitive drawing operations (`create_frame`, `create_text`,
  `set_fill_color`, `set_layout_mode`, …) rather than Figma's own
  `use_figma` Plugin-API sandbox, so the same design costs more calls;
- it has no `create_new_file`, so the Kimi run cannot open its own file;
- variables/tokens must be built out of what the plugin API exposes.

This is a real handicap on the *Figma craftsmanship* dimension (8% of the
scorecard). Score it honestly and note the cause; do not let it silently
depress the run's other scores.

## Kimi setup, as installed

    uv tool install kimi-cli          # binary lands at ~/.local/bin/kimi

`~/bin/kimi` still runs Joe's own `~/kimi-k3/kimi.py` chat helper — it shadows
the new one on PATH deliberately. The agentic CLI is exposed as **`kimi-code`**
(`~/bin/kimi-code`). Nothing about the existing `kimi` / `kimi-one` /
`kimi-chat` workflow changed.

`~/.kimi/config.toml` points the CLI at the Moonshot Open Platform with the
same `MOONSHOT_API_KEY` from `~/.zshenv`:

    [providers.moonshot]
    type = "openai_legacy"
    base_url = "https://api.moonshot.ai/v1"

    [models.kimi-k3]
    provider = "moonshot"
    model = "kimi-k3"

Models the key can reach: `kimi-k3`, `kimi-k2.6`, `kimi-k2.7-code`,
`kimi-k2.7-code-highspeed`. The benchmark uses `kimi-k3`.

`~/.kimi/mcp.json`:

    {
      "mcpServers": {
        "canva": { "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "mcp-remote", "https://mcp.canva.com/mcp"] },
        "figma": { "command": "bun",  "args": ["/Users/josephwaine/fractal/figma-bridge/src/talk_to_figma_mcp/server.ts"] }
      }
    }

Canva goes through `mcp-remote` because Kimi's built-in OAuth client sends
both a client secret and PKCE, which Canva rejects:

    Token exchange failed (400): "Client must not use multiple authentication methods"

`mcp-remote` negotiates cleanly and caches its tokens in `~/.mcp-auth/`.
Verified: 33 Canva tools, including `generate-design`, the editing-transaction
trio, `upload-asset-from-url` and `export-design`.

## Before a Kimi run — the one manual step

1. `bin/figma-socket.sh start` (websocket on :3055).
2. In Figma desktop: **Plugins → Development → Import plugin from manifest…**
   → `~/fractal/figma-bridge/src/cursor_mcp_plugin/manifest.json`
   (one-time; afterwards it is under Plugins → Development).
3. Create the empty design file for the run and run the plugin in it.
4. **Click Connect in the plugin panel.** Importing and running the plugin does
   not connect it. The panel must show `Connected to server in channel: <id>`.
5. Leave that file and the panel open for the whole run.

`join_channel` takes the channel id as a required argument, and that id is
generated by the plugin and displayed only in its panel — an agent has no way
to discover it. `bin/run-agent.sh <slug> kimi` reads the id back off
`~/fractal/figma-bridge/socket.log` (the `Type: join, Channel: <id>` line) and
passes it in the launch prompt, and refuses to launch if the plugin has never
joined. Ignore the "copy this to your mcp.json in Cursor" block in the panel —
that is upstream boilerplate; `~/.kimi/mcp.json` already points at the local
build.

